Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 21:04:49 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] WP-0.7, enforcement (cld-advisor's series 3, patch 6;
 built on b80c68991 with series 2 applied): A GATE READS ALL IT IS ABOUT --
 THE CONTROLS GATE (the seat's planted case PV16). The gate checked that the
 function a gate's control names is defined in the file it names, and nothing
 of the function: with every gate pointed at a skipped test whose only line
 was 'assert True is not False', it passed. Three things are now read, none by
 running a test, which is the suite's: (1) THE CONTROL IS NOT SKIPPED: no
 skip, skipif or xfail mark on it; no such mark on its module, and no skip at
 the module's import; no call that skips in its body. (2) THE CONTROL CAN
 FAIL: it is a test function at the top level of its file, with an assert, and
 not one in which no assert can fail (the class of patch 3). (3) A CONTROL
 DOES NOT LEAVE THE REGISTER WITHOUT A RECORDED ACT, the rule of series 2: a
 gate pointed at another test, however honest that test looks, is a row that
 left, and is named in LEFT_BY_NAME with its date and why. The gate therefore
 reads the past and joins the table of series 1 (4 gates ask which files are
 tracked, 20 read the past, 29 need no git; the floor in that table's lock
 moves from 30 to 29, with a dated note). On main all 53 controls pass the
 three, and the register has not changed since the anchor; in the last 600
 commits it was changed 14 times. WHAT THIS DOES NOT SHOW, and the case asked
 for: that a control turns ITS gate red. That takes a run with the gate made
 unable to fail; the seat's note has what such a run reads on main. The lock:
 five tests added to tests/test_a_gate_reads_all_it_is_about.py, with nine
 forms of a control that cannot control; on the commit before, four fail,
 three of them because the gate passes. 0 of 19.

